        id: 'html-semantic-q1',
        question: 'What are semantic HTML5 elements and why should you use them?',
        answer:
          'Semantic HTML5 elements are tags that convey meaning about the content they contain rather than just defining how it looks. Unlike generic containers such as <div> and <span>, semantic elements like <header>, <nav>, <main>, <article>, <section>, <aside>, and <footer> describe the role their content plays in the page structure.\n\nUsing semantic elements improves accessibility significantly. Screen readers rely on semantic markup to build an outline of the page and let users jump between sections quickly. When a screen reader encounters a <nav> element, it announces it as a navigation landmark, allowing users to skip directly to it.\n\nSearch engines also benefit because they can parse the document structure more intelligently. Google and other crawlers give weight to content within <main> and <article> elements, and proper heading hierarchy inside semantic sections helps establish topic structure for indexing.\n\nFrom a developer experience standpoint, semantic HTML makes code more readable and maintainable. When another developer sees <aside>, they immediately understand it contains supplementary content, whereas a <div class="sidebar"> requires reading the class name. Semantic elements also carry implicit ARIA roles\u2014<nav> maps to role="navigation", <main> to role="main"\u2014reducing the need for manual ARIA.\n\nFinally, semantic HTML is part of the HTML specification and requires no libraries or polyfills in modern browsers. It is the lowest-effort, highest-impact step toward inclusive, well-structured web pages.',
