        id: 'html-semantic-q2',
        question: 'What is the difference between <article> and <section>?',
        answer:
          'The <article> element represents a self-contained, independently distributable piece of content. Think of content that could be syndicated\u2014a blog post, a news story, a user comment, or a product card. Each article should make sense on its own, even if pulled out of the page and displayed elsewhere.\n\nThe <section> element represents a thematic grouping of content that is part of a larger whole. A section typically has a heading and groups related content within a page. For example, a landing page might have sections for "Features," "Pricing," and "Testimonials."\n\nA useful litmus test is the "syndication test": if the content would make sense in an RSS feed or as a standalone card, use <article>. If it is a logical grouping within the current page, use <section>. You can nest sections inside articles\u2014an article about a tutorial might have sections for each step.\n\nYou can also nest articles inside sections. A "Comments" section of a blog post would use <section> to group comments, and each individual comment could be an <article> because comments are self-contained units.\n\nBoth elements carry implicit ARIA roles: "article" for <article> and "region" for <section>. A <section> should generally have an accessible name via a heading or aria-label; otherwise it is treated as a generic container by assistive technologies.',
