        answer: 'HTML5 introduced numerous input types beyond the classic text, password, and checkbox fields. The type="email" input provides built-in email format validation and triggers an optimized keyboard on mobile. The type="url" input similarly validates URL format and shows a keyboard with .com and / keys on mobile.\n\nThe type="number" input restricts entry to numeric values and provides spinner buttons. It accepts min, max, and step attributes. However, number inputs are not suitable for all numeric data\u2014phone numbers, ZIP codes, and credit card numbers should use type="tel" or type="text" with inputmode="numeric" because they are identifiers, not quantities.\n\nDate-related inputs (type="date", type="time", type="datetime-local") provide native date pickers in supporting browsers. They return ISO-formatted strings and respect min/max constraints. Their appearance varies across browsers, but they are keyboard-navigable and screen reader-friendly out of the box.\n\nThe type="range" input renders a slider control ideal for settings where exact values matter less. The type="color" provides a native color picker returning hex values. The type="search" is similar to text but may display a clear button.\n\nThe type="file" input enables file uploads and supports the accept attribute for filtering file types and the multiple attribute for multi-file selection. Using the correct type provides free validation, improves mobile UX with appropriate keyboards, and makes forms more accessible.',
