        answer: 'HTML5 provides built-in form validation through attributes that define constraints on input values. The browser evaluates these constraints on submit and prevents submission if any field is invalid, displaying native error messages.\n\nThe "required" attribute marks a field as mandatory. The "pattern" attribute accepts a regular expression that the value must match. Unlike JavaScript regex, the pattern is auto-anchored to match the entire value. The "title" attribute alongside "pattern" provides a human-readable description for the validation tooltip.\n\nNumeric constraints include "min" and "max" for value ranges and "step" for valid increments. String length constraints use "minlength" and "maxlength". The "type" attribute itself provides validation\u2014type="email" validates email format, type="url" validates URL format.\n\nBrowsers apply CSS pseudo-classes based on validity: :valid, :invalid, :required, :optional, :in-range, :out-of-range. Be careful with :invalid\u2014it applies immediately, showing error styling before user interaction. Use :invalid:not(:placeholder-shown) or :user-invalid (where supported) to avoid premature error display.\n\nTo disable native validation and implement custom UX, add "novalidate" to the <form>. Then use the Constraint Validation API in JavaScript to check validity programmatically while controlling how and when errors are displayed.',
