        id: 'html-forms-q5',
        question: 'What is the difference between native form submission and JavaScript form submission?',
        answer: 'Traditional HTML form submission uses action and method attributes. The action specifies the URL and method the HTTP method (GET or POST). The browser serializes data, navigates to the action URL, and the server responds with a new page. The enctype attribute controls encoding: "application/x-www-form-urlencoded" (default), "multipart/form-data" (for files), or "text/plain".\n\nJavaScript submission intercepts the submit event with addEventListener, calls preventDefault(), and sends data via fetch or XMLHttpRequest. This is standard in SPAs because it avoids page reloads, enables custom loading states, and supports JSON.\n\nThe native approach is simpler and works without JavaScript, making it solid for progressive enhancement. It also has built-in accessibility: the browser handles focus management after submission and displays native validation messages. However, it causes a full page navigation.\n\nJavaScript submission offers flexibility: real-time validation, inline error messages, JSON payloads, response handling without navigation, and optimistic UI patterns. The tradeoff is that you must handle error states, loading indicators, and accessibility manually.\n\nBest practice is to combine both: set valid action and method as a fallback, then enhance with JavaScript. Use formaction and formmethod on submit buttons for different endpoints. Frameworks like Remix build on this progressive enhancement pattern.',
        shortAnswer: 'Native submission via action/method navigates to a new page and works without JavaScript. JavaScript submission intercepts the event, uses fetch, avoids reloads, and enables custom UX. Best practice is progressive enhancement: set action/method as fallback and enhance with JavaScript.',
