        id: 'html-forms-q6',
        question: 'How do you implement custom validation styling without JavaScript?',
        answer: 'CSS provides powerful pseudo-classes for styling form validation states without JavaScript. :valid matches elements satisfying constraints, while :invalid matches those violating them. Combined with :required and :optional, you can create sophisticated validation feedback purely through CSS.\n\nThe primary challenge is timing: :invalid applies on page load, so required empty fields show error styling before user interaction. Several solutions exist. :placeholder-shown matches inputs whose placeholder is visible, so input:invalid:not(:placeholder-shown) only shows errors after the user has entered and cleared a value. The newer :user-invalid (supported in modern browsers) only matches after user interaction.\n\n:focus-within on a form group shows validation during active editing. :in-range and :out-of-range work specifically with number and date inputs having min/max constraints.\n\nFor visual feedback, use CSS content or background images for checkmarks/warning icons. The + adjacent sibling selector shows/hides error messages based on input state: input:invalid + .error-message { display: block; }.\n\nWhile CSS-only validation works well for simple forms, complex scenarios like cross-field validation or server-side errors still require JavaScript. CSS validation is best used as a progressive enhancement layer on top of HTML validation attributes.',
        shortAnswer: 'CSS pseudo-classes like :valid, :invalid, :required, :in-range, and :user-invalid enable validation styling without JavaScript. Use :invalid:not(:placeholder-shown) to avoid showing errors before interaction. Adjacent sibling selectors can toggle error message visibility.',
