        id: 'html-responsive-q2',
        question: 'How do srcset and sizes work for responsive images?',
        answer: 'The srcset and sizes attributes enable the browser to choose the most appropriate image file based on screen size, pixel density, and network conditions. They work at the HTML parsing stage, allowing the browser to start downloading the right image before CSS is parsed.\n\nsrcset provides a list of image sources with descriptors. Width descriptors (e.g., "image-800.jpg 800w") tell the browser the intrinsic pixel width of each file. Pixel density descriptors (e.g., "image-2x.jpg 2x") specify the intended device pixel ratio. Width descriptors are more flexible with sizes.\n\nsizes tells the browser how wide the image will display at different viewport widths, using media conditions and length values. For example, sizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 50vw, 33vw" means: up to 600px use full width, up to 1200px use half, otherwise use a third.\n\nThe browser combines srcset widths with sizes and device pixel ratio to calculate which image fits best. On a 400px-wide device with 2x DPR where sizes says 100vw, the browser looks for an image close to 800w (400 CSS pixels times 2 DPR).\n\nImportantly, srcset is a suggestion, not a directive. The browser may choose a smaller image on slow connections. This differs from <picture>, which gives the developer explicit control.',
        shortAnswer: 'srcset provides multiple image files with width descriptors (e.g., 800w), while sizes tells the browser how wide the image will display at different viewports. The browser multiplies display width by device pixel ratio to select the best image. It is a hint-based system\u2014the browser retains discretion.',
