        question: 'What is the <picture> element and how does it differ from srcset?',
        answer: 'The <picture> element provides explicit art direction control\u2014the developer, not the browser, decides which image to display based on media conditions. While srcset allows the browser to choose from similar images at different resolutions, <picture> enables showing entirely different images at different breakpoints.\n\n<picture> contains one or more <source> elements and a required <img> fallback. Each <source> can specify media conditions, image formats (type attribute), and srcset. The browser evaluates sources in order and uses the first matching one.\n\nArt direction is the primary use case. A wide panoramic banner on desktop might need a portrait crop on mobile. With srcset alone, you can only switch resolutions. With <picture>, you serve completely different crops.\n\nFormat selection is another major use case. Serve modern formats like WebP or AVIF to supporting browsers while falling back to JPEG: <source type="image/avif" srcset="photo.avif"> followed by <source type="image/webp" srcset="photo.webp"> with <img src="photo.jpg"> as fallback.\n\nA key difference: <picture> sources are deterministic\u2014the browser must use the first matching source. srcset is preferred for simple resolution switching because it lets the browser consider network speed and data saver mode.',
