        id: 'html-responsive-q4',
        question: 'How do media queries work in HTML (outside of CSS)?',
        answer: 'While media queries are most commonly used in CSS, HTML provides elements and attributes that accept media conditions for responsive behavior at the document level. These execute before stylesheets are applied, enabling earlier decisions about resource loading.\n\nThe <link> element accepts a media attribute to conditionally load stylesheets: <link rel="stylesheet" href="print.css" media="print">. Note the stylesheet is still downloaded but deprioritized. For truly conditional loading, JavaScript or dynamic import is needed.\n\nThe <source> element inside <picture> accepts a media attribute for art direction. The <source> inside <video> and <audio> also supports media.\n\nThe <style> element supports media to scope its rules: <style media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)">...</style>. This is equivalent to wrapping rules in a @media block in CSS.\n\nAn emerging pattern uses media attributes with <link rel="preload"> to conditionally preload resources: <link rel="preload" as="image" href="hero-large.jpg" media="(min-width: 1024px)">. This loads the large hero image only on desktop.',
