        id: 'html-apis-q2',
        question: 'How does the HTML Drag and Drop API work?',
        answer: 'The API centers around drag events (dragstart, drag, dragenter, dragover, dragleave, drop, dragend) and the DataTransfer object. To make an element draggable, add draggable="true".\n\nOn dragstart, use event.dataTransfer.setData(format, data) to attach data. The format is a MIME type string. Set effectAllowed to control which drop effects are permitted.\n\nThe drop target must handle dragover by calling event.preventDefault()\u2014this is required because the default behavior rejects drops. Set event.dataTransfer.dropEffect in dragover. The drop event fires when the user releases over a valid target; call preventDefault() and retrieve data with getData().\n\nThe DataTransfer object also supports file drops from the desktop via event.dataTransfer.files, enabling file upload UIs without a file input. The items property provides a modern interface supporting both strings and files.\n\nAccessibility is a major concern. The API is mouse-oriented with no keyboard support. You must implement keyboard alternatives (selecting items and using shortcuts to move them) and ensure screen readers understand the operation using ARIA live regions.',
        shortAnswer: 'The Drag and Drop API uses draggable="true" on source elements and handles drag events on targets. DataTransfer carries data between source and target. Drop targets must call preventDefault() on dragover. Always provide keyboard alternatives for accessibility.',
