        id: 'html-apis-q3',
        question: 'What is the History API and how is it used for client-side routing?',
        answer: 'The History API enables client-side routing without page reloads. The key methods are history.pushState(state, title, url) and history.replaceState(state, title, url). pushState adds a new entry to the browser history stack, while replaceState modifies the current entry.\n\nThe state parameter is a serializable object associated with the history entry, available via history.state and passed to the popstate event. The title is largely ignored by browsers. The url changes the address bar without triggering a page load\u2014it must be same-origin.\n\nThe popstate event fires when the user navigates with back/forward buttons. Importantly, popstate does not fire when pushState/replaceState is called\u2014only on navigation to a previously pushed state. Your navigate function must manually call the route handler after pushState.\n\nClient-side routing frameworks (React Router, Vue Router) build on this: they intercept link clicks, call pushState, render appropriate components, and handle popstate for back/forward. They add scroll restoration, lazy loading, and transitions on top.\n\nA critical consideration: pushState URLs must be handled by the server. If a user refreshes a client-side route like /about, the server must respond with the same HTML shell, not a 404. SPAs typically configure catch-all server routes.',
        shortAnswer: 'The History API (pushState, replaceState, popstate) enables client-side routing by modifying the URL without page reloads. pushState adds a history entry; replaceState modifies the current one. popstate handles browser back/forward. Server catch-all routes are needed for direct URL access.',
