        id: 'html-templates-q1',
        question: 'What is the <template> element and how does it differ from hidden DOM content?',
        answer: 'The <template> element holds HTML content that is not rendered on load. Its content is parsed but completely inert: not part of the rendered DOM, images do not load, scripts do not execute, CSS does not apply, and form elements are not submittable.\n\nThis differs fundamentally from hiding content with CSS (display: none) or the hidden attribute. Hidden content is fully in the DOM: images load, scripts execute, event listeners are active. A template is truly inert\u2014it exists in a separate DocumentFragment via template.content.\n\nTo use a template, clone the fragment with cloneNode(true), modify the clone, and append it to the live DOM. Each clone is independent. This makes templates ideal for stamp-out patterns needing many similar DOM structures.\n\nCompared to innerHTML, templates are safer because content is pre-parsed and does not execute scripts when cloned. innerHTML can introduce XSS if user data is interpolated. Templates also outperform repeated innerHTML because the browser parses once and cloning is fast.\n\nTemplates are foundational to Web Components. When defining a custom element with Shadow DOM, the common pattern is to define markup in a <template>, then clone it into the shadow root during construction.',
