        id: 'html-templates-q2',
        question: 'How do you clone and populate templates dynamically?',
        answer: 'Cloning involves: get a reference to the template, clone its content property (a DocumentFragment), modify the clone, and insert it into the live DOM. cloneNode(true) creates a deep copy.\n\nOnce cloned, the fragment is a regular DocumentFragment you can query and modify. Use querySelector to find elements and set textContent, attributes, or event listeners. After populating, append to a container with appendChild.\n\nFor list rendering, iterate over data, clone for each item, populate, and either append directly or collect all clones in a DocumentFragment to minimize reflows.\n\nAfter appending a DocumentFragment, it becomes empty. If you need to reference inserted nodes later, query the container or store references before appending. cloneNode(true) copies DOM structure but not JavaScript event listeners added with addEventListener\u2014only inline handlers are cloned.\n\nA more advanced pattern uses the <template> for overall structure and JavaScript for dynamic parts, keeping structure declarative while allowing programmatic data binding.',
        shortAnswer: 'Clone templates via template.content.cloneNode(true), query the fragment with querySelector to populate elements, and append to the DOM. For batch rendering, collect clones in a DocumentFragment first. Event listeners are not cloned\u2014attach them after insertion.',
