        question: 'What are slots in HTML and how do they enable content projection?',
        answer: 'Slots are a Web Components feature allowing consumers of a custom element to inject content into predefined placeholders within the shadow DOM. Named slots use the name attribute: <slot name="header"></slot>. Consumers project content with the slot attribute: <my-component><h2 slot="header">Title</h2></my-component>. The default slot receives unassigned children.\n\nSlots provide fallback content: content inside the <slot> element displays when nothing is projected. For example, <slot name="icon">default icon</slot> shows the default when no content is provided.\n\nThe ::slotted() CSS pseudo-element styles projected content, but only direct children of the slot, not deeper descendants. The component can listen for the slotchange event to react when slotted content changes.\n\nSlots do not move DOM nodes\u2014slotted content remains in the light DOM but is visually rendered at the slot position. This matters for styling (content inherits from its original location) and querying (elements are still queryable from light DOM). assignedNodes() and assignedElements() on a slot return projected content.\n\nThe analogy is content projection in Angular or children in React\u2014slots provide a standardized, browser-native version of this pattern.',
