        question: 'What are the benefits of using <template> over innerHTML for dynamic content?',
        answer: 'Security is the most critical benefit. innerHTML parses strings as HTML, risking XSS with user-provided data. Templates are pre-parsed HTML populated by setting textContent, which escapes HTML entities automatically.\n\nPerformance: innerHTML causes the browser to parse HTML strings every time. A template is parsed once on page load; cloneNode(true) is significantly faster than re-parsing, especially in loops creating many similar elements. Setting innerHTML also destroys existing child nodes and their event listeners.\n\nMaintainability improves because template HTML is in document markup with IDE support, linting, and syntax highlighting. Complex HTML via string concatenation is error-prone and impossible for IDEs to validate.\n\nTemplates preserve DOM correctness. innerHTML requires valid HTML in the current context\u2014inserting <tr> elements into a <div> fails because the browser strips them. Templates parse in an inert context without such restrictions.\n\nThe main drawback is verbosity for simple cases. Creating a single element with one class is more code with templates. For simple insertions without user data, innerHTML can be acceptable, but templates are the better default for repeated or data-driven content.',
        shortAnswer: 'Templates are safer than innerHTML (no XSS risk via textContent), faster (parsed once, cloned many times), more maintainable (HTML in markup with IDE support), and more correct (no context-dependent parsing issues). innerHTML is simpler for trivial cases but introduces security risks with user data.',
