        id: 'html-microdata-q1',
        question: 'What is HTML Microdata and how do itemscope, itemtype, and itemprop work?',
        answer: 'HTML Microdata embeds structured, machine-readable data in HTML using three core attributes: itemscope, itemtype, and itemprop. This data is invisible to users but can be parsed by search engines and automated tools to understand content meaning.\n\nitemscope creates a new item\u2014a group of name-value pairs. When added to an element, its descendants contain information about a particular item. itemtype specifies the vocabulary, typically a schema.org URL. It must be on the same element as itemscope.\n\nitemprop names a property. The value is extracted differently by element type: for <meta> it is the content attribute; for <a> and <link>, the href; for <img>, the src; for <time>, datetime; for most others, text content. This flexibility allows microdata without changing visible content.\n\nNested items combine itemprop with itemscope on the same element. A Product item might have itemprop="offers" that itself has itemscope and itemtype="https://schema.org/Offer". This creates a tree structure.\n\nMultiple elements with the same itemprop create arrays. When data cannot be displayed visually, use <meta> with content for machine-readable values or <link> with href for URL values.',
        shortAnswer: 'Microdata uses itemscope (creates an item), itemtype (specifies schema.org vocabulary), and itemprop (names a property). Values come from element-specific attributes (content, href, src, datetime) or text content. Items can be nested by combining itemprop with itemscope.',
