        id: 'html-microdata-q3',
        question: 'How does microdata compare to JSON-LD?',
        answer: 'Microdata is inline\u2014it annotates existing HTML with attributes. JSON-LD is a separate block in a <script type="application/ld+json"> tag that describes data independently of HTML structure.\n\nJSON-LD is recommended by Google. Its advantages: complete separation of concerns, easier maintenance (single JSON block vs scattered attributes), simpler tooling (standard JSON, server-side generation), support for complex relationships beyond DOM hierarchy, and easier dynamic generation.\n\nMicrodata advantages: inherently tied to visible content, reducing risk of structured data diverging from what users see. It does not require a separate data block. For simple schemas on content pages (articles, products), microdata feels natural.\n\nRDFa is a third option using attributes like typeof and property. It is more powerful for complex graph relationships but also more complex. JSON-LD and microdata cover the vast majority of use cases.\n\nGoogle treats all three formats equally for SEO. The choice is often technical: server-rendered pages may favor microdata for co-location, while SPAs may favor JSON-LD for DOM independence.',
