        question: 'What are rich snippets and how does structured data enable them?',
        answer: 'Rich snippets are enhanced search results displaying additional information beyond title, URL, and description. They are generated when search engines find valid structured data. Examples include star ratings, cooking times, event dates, FAQ accordions, and breadcrumb paths.\n\nSearch engines parse structured data and, for supported schemas meeting quality guidelines, render enhanced results. Not all structured data produces rich snippets\u2014Google supports specific types including Product, Recipe, Article, Event, FAQ, HowTo, LocalBusiness, BreadcrumbList, and Review.\n\nRich snippets improve CTR by 20-30% on average. They help users make decisions from search results\u2014seeing a product rating and price before clicking.\n\nValidation is essential. The Rich Results Test (search.google.com/test/rich-results) checks eligibility. Schema.org Markup Validator checks general compliance. Common issues: missing required properties, incorrect value types, data not matching visible content.\n\nImplementing structured data does not guarantee rich snippets. Search engines decide based on page quality, data accuracy, and algorithmic factors. Structured data is a guideline, not a contract.',
