        answer: 'Schema.org is a shared vocabulary for structured data created by Google, Microsoft, Yahoo, and Yandex. It provides a language for describing things on the web\u2014products, people, events, recipes, articles, and hundreds of other types.\n\nStructured data enables rich results\u2014enhanced search listings with star ratings, prices, FAQ dropdowns, or event dates. These can improve click-through rates by 20-30%. It also feeds knowledge panels and knowledge graph entries.\n\nWhile Google states structured data is not a direct ranking factor, it indirectly affects rankings through improved CTR, reduced bounce rates, and better content understanding.\n\nSchema.org defines a type hierarchy (Thing, CreativeWork, Article, NewsArticle) where child types inherit parent properties. Each type has required and recommended properties. Google documents which types support rich results.\n\nStructured data should accurately reflect visible page content. Google has penalized sites where structured data contained claims not supported by what users see.',
