        question: 'What is the difference between async and defer on script tags?',
        answer: 'Without async or defer, a <script src="..."> blocks HTML parsing while the script downloads and executes. Both attributes download in parallel with parsing, but differ in execution timing.\n\ndefer downloads in parallel but delays execution until HTML is fully parsed (just before DOMContentLoaded). Multiple deferred scripts execute in document order. Ideal for scripts needing the full DOM and depending on each other, like your main application bundle.\n\nasync downloads in parallel and executes immediately when ready, pausing HTML parsing. Multiple async scripts execute in download-completion order\u2014order is not guaranteed. Ideal for independent scripts like analytics and ad trackers.\n\nFor ES module scripts (<script type="module">), defer is the default. Adding async to a module changes it to execute as soon as its module graph resolves.\n\nCommon pattern: defer for your main app script, async for independent third-party scripts. Scripts in <head> with defer do not block rendering and execute after parsing. For inline scripts (without src), async and defer have no effect.',
