        question: 'What are resource hints and when do you use each?',
        answer: 'Resource hints are <link> elements that inform the browser about resources it will need. Each operates at a different level of the loading pipeline.\n\ndns-prefetch performs DNS resolution for a domain (20-120ms savings). Use for third-party domains: CDNs, analytics, font providers. Syntax: <link rel="dns-prefetch" href="https://cdn.example.com">.\n\npreconnect does DNS + TCP + TLS handshake (100-500ms savings). Use for critical third-party origins with early requests. Add crossorigin for CORS resources like fonts.\n\npreload fetches a specific resource with high priority for the current page. The as attribute specifies type (style, script, font, image, fetch) for correct prioritization. Use for critical fonts, above-fold images, or CSS. Do not set crossorigin incorrectly or the resource downloads twice.\n\nprefetch requests a resource with low priority for future navigation. Downloaded during idle time. Use for resources on the likely next page.\n\nOrder in HTML matters: place dns-prefetch and preconnect early in <head>. Place preload before the resources they accelerate. Prefetch can go anywhere.',
        shortAnswer: 'dns-prefetch resolves DNS early. preconnect adds TCP/TLS handshake. preload fetches specific critical resources for the current page. prefetch fetches for future navigations with low priority. Order: dns-prefetch for all third-party domains, preconnect for critical ones, preload for key resources, prefetch for next-page resources.',
