        id: 'html-performance-q3',
        question: 'What is the critical rendering path?',
        answer: 'The critical rendering path (CRP) is the sequence of steps to convert HTML, CSS, and JavaScript into rendered pixels: construct the DOM from HTML, construct the CSSOM from CSS, combine into the render tree, calculate layout, and paint.\n\nCSS is render-blocking by default: the browser will not paint until all CSS in <head> is downloaded and parsed. Rendering without CSS would show FOUC (Flash of Unstyled Content). To minimize blocking, keep critical CSS small, inline above-the-fold CSS, and load non-critical CSS asynchronously.\n\nJavaScript without async/defer is parser-blocking: it stops HTML parsing while downloading and executing. This delays DOM construction. defer allows parsing to continue; async allows download to continue.\n\nResource order in <head> matters. The browser preload scanner looks ahead for resources to fetch but cannot discover resources loaded by JavaScript or CSS (like background images or font-face URLs).\n\nOptimization strategies: minimize render-blocking CSS, inline critical CSS, use defer/async on scripts, preload critical undiscoverable resources (fonts, dynamic CSS), and ensure LCP element resources are discoverable early in the document.',
        shortAnswer: 'The CRP converts HTML/CSS/JS into pixels through DOM construction, CSSOM construction, render tree, layout, and paint. CSS is render-blocking; JS without async/defer is parser-blocking. Optimize by inlining critical CSS, deferring scripts, preloading key resources.',
