        id: 'html-performance-q5',
        question: 'What is fetchpriority and how does it improve performance?',
        answer: 'fetchpriority hints the browser about relative resource priority with "high", "low", or "auto" (default). It applies to <img>, <script>, <link>, <iframe>, and the fetch() API.\n\nThe browser assigns default priorities by type and position. fetchpriority lets you override when you know better. The most impactful use: fetchpriority="high" on the LCP image. By default, images start low-priority until the browser determines they are in-viewport. fetchpriority="high" immediately promotes it, potentially saving hundreds of milliseconds.\n\nfetchpriority="low" deprioritizes non-critical resources competing for bandwidth. Below-fold images with loading="lazy" can benefit from fetchpriority="low". Non-essential scripts can also be deprioritized.\n\nIt works with preload links: <link rel="preload" href="hero.jpg" as="image" fetchpriority="high">. When preloading multiple resources, priorities ensure the most critical downloads first.\n\nfetchpriority is a hint, not a directive. The browser considers it alongside its own heuristics and may adjust based on network conditions.',
