        id: 'html-forms-a11y-q4',
        question: 'What is the role of aria-describedby in forms?',
        answer: 'aria-describedby creates a programmatic association between a form control and supplementary text. Unlike a label (which names the control), descriptions provide additional context\u2014instructions, format hints, requirements, or error messages. Screen readers announce the description after the label and input type.\n\nCommon uses include: password requirements ("Must be at least 8 characters with one uppercase letter"), input format hints ("Format: MM/DD/YYYY"), field-specific instructions ("As it appears on your ID"), and error messages.\n\naria-describedby accepts space-separated IDs to reference multiple elements. The referenced elements are read in the order listed. A password field might reference both the requirements list and an error message: aria-describedby="pwd-requirements pwd-error".\n\nThe referenced elements do not need to be visible. You can visually hide supplementary text while keeping it accessible. However, visible descriptions are preferred because they help all users, not just screen reader users.\n\nThe key difference from aria-labelledby: labels name the control ("Email address"), descriptions provide supplementary information ("We will never share your email"). Use aria-labelledby for the primary identifier and aria-describedby for additional context.',
        shortAnswer: 'aria-describedby associates supplementary text (instructions, hints, errors) with a form control. Screen readers announce it after the label. It accepts space-separated IDs for multiple descriptions. Unlike aria-labelledby which names the control, aria-describedby provides additional context.',
